(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-2-immunity-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Immunity Quiz 2 — Clinical Pathophysiology I","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which antibodies are usually present in the plasma of a group O person?","choices":["Anti-A without anti-B","Anti-B without anti-A","Neither anti-A nor anti-B","Anti-A and anti-B"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Group O red cells carry neither A nor B antigen, and group O plasma usually carries both anti-A and anti-B. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which function of immunity removes cellular debris?","choices":["Homeostasis","Defense","Surveillance","Memory"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Immunity is a state of protection with three functions: defense (resistance to invaders), surveillance (of self for mutant or cancerous cells) and homeostasis (removing cellular debris). — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which mechanism defines type II hypersensitivity?","choices":["IgE cross-linked on mast cells","Circulating complexes depositing in vessels","IgG or IgM binding cell or tissue antigens","Sensitized T cells releasing cytokines"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In type II hypersensitivity IgG or IgM binds antigens on cells or within tissues, and injury follows through phagocytosis, complement or antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What keeps lymph moving along the larger lymphatic vessels?","choices":["Contraction of the heart alone","Gravity draining it down toward the feet","Muscle movement, breathing and arterial pulsation","Cilia beating along the lymphatic lumen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Skeletal muscle movement, pressure changes in the thorax during breathing and the pulsations of adjacent arteries help propel lymph, while the vessel's valves keep it moving forward. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What forms when the vesicle holding an engulfed microbe fuses with a lysosome?","choices":["A phagolysosome","A residual body","A pseudopod","A membrane attack complex"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The phagocytic vesicle (phagosome) fuses with a lysosome to form the phagolysosome, where lysosomal enzymes kill and digest the microbe. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"From which precursor do lymphocytes arise?","choices":["Myeloblast","Monoblast","Lymphoblast","Megakaryocyte"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In blood cell formation the hematopoietic stem cell gives rise to the lymphoblast, which becomes the lymphocyte. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What defines hypersensitivity?","choices":["Any immune response to a foreign antigen","Failure to produce enough antibody","Immune reactions that injure the body","Infection with a pyogenic organism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypersensitivity is when immune reactions, either humoral or cellular, result in injury to the body; four types are seen. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which cell, when seen on a stained smear, is NOT a normal constituent of peripheral blood?","choices":["Monocyte","Small lymphocyte","Plasma cell","Eosinophil"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A plasma cell is not a normal constituent of peripheral blood; it is shown beside the nongranular leukocytes for comparison. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which immunoglobulin crosses the placenta?","choices":["IgM","IgG","IgA","IgE"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. IgG crosses the placenta and provides maternal protection; it is also the most abundant serum class. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 44, 46"},{"q":"Which pair of antibody effects acts on a soluble toxin?","choices":["Opsonization and lysis","Agglutination and opsonization","Agglutination and lysis","Neutralization and precipitation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A toxin is soluble, so antibodies neutralize its action and cross-link toxin molecules into precipitating complexes; viruses are neutralized and agglutinated, bacteria opsonized. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 54, 58"},{"q":"Which components assemble the membrane attack complex?","choices":["C5b with C6–C9","C1 with C4 and C2","C3a and C5a","Factor B and factor D"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. C5b initiates assembly of the terminal components C6–C9, and the assembled complex forms pores in a susceptible target membrane. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which defect underlies X-linked agammaglobulinemia?","choices":["Abnormal thymic development","Profound T-cell dysfunction","Medication-induced loss of function","Impaired B-cell maturation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. X-linked agammaglobulinemia is impaired B-cell maturation with very low antibodies, and is associated with recurrent pyogenic bacterial infections. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which of these organs belongs to the lymphatic system?","choices":["Thyroid gland","Thymus gland","Adrenal gland","Pancreas"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The labeled lymphatic system includes the thymus gland together with the tonsils, spleen, appendix, lymph vessels and the lymph nodes of the neck, axilla, groin and knee. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which hypersensitivity type is mediated by IgE fixed on mast cells?","choices":["Type I","Type II","Type III","Type IV"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Type I (anaphylactic, immediate) hypersensitivity: IgE made at a first exposure becomes fixed on mast cells and basophils, and re-exposure releases histamine and other substances. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 60, 61"},{"q":"What commonly starts the classical complement pathway?","choices":["Lectins binding microbial sugars","C1 binding antigen-bound IgM or IgG","C3 activating on permissive surfaces","C5b inserting into a membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The classical pathway commonly begins when C1 recognizes antigen-bound IgM or IgG, though some activation occurs without antibody. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which T cells limit immune activation and maintain self-tolerance?","choices":["Cytotoxic T cells","Helper T cells","Memory T cells","Regulatory T cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Regulatory T cells limit immune activation and maintain self-tolerance; many are CD4 cells expressing FOXP3. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"After a FIRST exposure to an antigen, which antibody class rises first?","choices":["IgG","IgA","IgM","IgE"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. On the primary response time course IgM rises and peaks first, with a smaller, later IgG curve; on second exposure IgG dominates. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 37, 46"},{"q":"What makes up a basic antibody unit?","choices":["One heavy and one light chain","Four identical heavy chains","Two heavy chains alone","Two heavy and two light chains"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A basic antibody unit has two heavy chains and two light chains, connected by disulfide bonds. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the main role of CD4 helper T cells?","choices":["Killing infected cells with perforin","Coordinating humoral and cellular responses","Secreting antibodies into plasma","Limiting activation to keep self-tolerance"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. CD4 helper T cells coordinate cell-mediated and humoral responses, using cytokines and cell contact to regulate other immune populations. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 24, 30"},{"q":"Which immunoglobulin is usually secreted as a pentamer?","choices":["IgA","IgM","IgG","IgD"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. IgM is usually secreted as a pentamer with a J chain, and is a monomer on the B-cell surface. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 44, 45"},{"q":"Which cells actually produce antibodies?","choices":["Plasma cells","Naive B cells","Helper T cells","Memory B cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In humoral immunity B cells are transformed into plasma cells, which are the actual antibody-producing cells. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 22, 24"},{"q":"Which defenses belong to innate immunity?","choices":["Complement and interferons","Memory B cells from a past exposure","Antibodies left by a past vaccine","Cytotoxic T cells with specific receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Innate immunity acts without prior sensitization and includes skin and mucosal barriers, phagocytes, complement and interferons, with natural killer cells as its cytotoxic defense. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Where do some resident tissue macrophages come from?","choices":["From monocytes entering inflamed tissue","From lymphoblasts maturing in the thymus","They develop before birth and renew locally","From B cells after antigen exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Some resident macrophages develop before birth and renew locally, while circulating monocytes add more during inflammation. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"How do natural killer cells differ from cytotoxic T cells?","choices":["They mature in the thymus","They secrete antibodies","They need no specific antigen","They need a prior exposure first"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Unlike cytotoxic T cells, natural killer cells do not need to recognize a specific antigen; they target tumor cells and protect against many infectious microbes. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Why is severe combined immunodeficiency called combined?","choices":["T-cell and B-cell immunity both fail","It combines primary and secondary causes","It affects both complement and antibody","It joins allergy with immune deficiency"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Severe combined immunodeficiency is profound T-cell dysfunction with impaired B-cell immunity; it may present in early infancy or be found by newborn screening. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What is opsonization?","choices":["Clumping of particles into a lattice","Pore formation in a target membrane","Blocking a toxin from its target","Coating that eases ingestion by phagocytes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In opsonization antibody joins antigen on the cell's surface, and this coating facilitates ingestion of the antigen by a phagocyte. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What does immunofixation add to serum protein electrophoresis?","choices":["The heavy- and light-chain types","Separation of albumin from globulins","A measure of complement activity","A count of CD4 helper T cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Electrophoresis separates serum proteins into albumin and globulin fractions by migration in an electric field; immunofixation characterizes the immunoglobulin heavy- and light-chain types. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Why does impaired helper T-cell function weaken antibody responses?","choices":["Helper T cells secrete the antibodies","Helper T cells form the B-cell receptor","Helper T cells carry antibody to tissue","Helper signals drive B-cell differentiation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Helper signals support B-cell differentiation and antibody production, so losing them weakens the antibody response even though plasma cells do the secreting. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which antibody fragment carries the effector functions?","choices":["Fab","Fc","Light-chain variable region","Hinge region"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fc contains the heavy-chain constant regions involved in effector functions; the complement-binding site lies on its CH2 domain. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 41, 42"},{"q":"Which three main functions does complement serve?","choices":["Antibody secretion, memory, opsonization","Opsonization, inflammation, membrane attack","Antigen presentation, lysis, memory","Neutralization, agglutination, precipitation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Complement opsonizes targets for phagocytes, generates inflammatory mediators that recruit and activate immune cells, and forms the membrane attack complex that damages susceptible membranes. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 67, 75"}]};
